 ```
 
+##### onRetry(context)
+
+Type: `Function`
+
+Callback invoked only when another attempt is going to happen, right before the wait. It is not called for the final failure, so it suits retry metrics. Receives the same context object as `onFailedAttempt`.
+
+The function is called _after_ `onFailedAttempt`, `shouldConsumeRetry`, and `shouldRetry`.
+
+The function is _not_ called when no retry follows: on `AbortError`, on an abort through `signal`, on `TypeError` (except network errors), when `shouldRetry` returns `false`, or if `retries` or `maxRetryTime` are exhausted.
+
+The function can return a promise, which is awaited before the wait starts. If the function throws, all retries will be aborted and the original promise will reject with the thrown error.
+
+```js
+import pRetry from 'p-retry';
+
+const run = async () => { … };
+
+const result = await pRetry(run, {
+	onRetry: ({error, attemptNumber}) => {
+		metrics.increment('retries', {reason: error.name, attemptNumber});
+	}
+});
+```
+
 ##### shouldRetry(context)
 
 Type: `Function`
@@ -206,6 +230,15 @@ Delays are also capped at the largest value a timer can hold, 2147483647ms, abou
 
 A fractional `maxTimeout` rounds up to a whole millisecond. A `maxTimeout` of `0` schedules no timer, like `minTimeout: 0`. See the warning on `minTimeout`.
 
+##### initialDelay
+
+Type: `number`\
+Default: `0`
+
+The number of milliseconds to wait before the first attempt. Nothing changes unless it is set.
+
+It is not counted towards `maxRetryTime`, which is measured from the first attempt. It is capped at 2147483647ms like the other delays. If `signal` aborts during the wait, the promise rejects with the abort reason and `input` is never called.
+
 ##### randomize
 
 Type: `boolean`\
